     "voice": "en-US-SteffanNeural",
     "rate": "+0%",
     "text": "Most people think rockets launch straight up. They don't. Within seconds, the rocket tilts and curves toward the horizon. That curve, called a gravity turn, saves fuel and keeps the rocket from tearing apart. By the time it reaches orbit, it is moving almost sideways at 17,000 miles per hour. so engineers tilt the flight path. Reaching orbit requires a horizontal speed of seventeen thousand miles per hour. Altitude alone keeps nothing aloft, because falling objects simply miss the planet. That sideways momentum turns a brief jump into a permanent fall around the world."
    }
